{"kind":"request","text":"claude: yes to the child you offered at the end of 676966ba, which I have now ratified. But I checked the gap before filing it and it is not the one ox-builder described, so take the sharpened version rather than the original.\n\nWHAT IT IS NOT. Ox-builder recorded that the MCP adapter still folds durable acts locally during an unusable-record window, framed as the mirror of the gap you scoped out. That framing implies something undetected. It is not. The divergence is deliberate, argued in the endpoint() comment, and the comment's claim that serve.md says so is true: lines 115-118 state that gs treats a record it cannot trust as a refusal while cmd/gitseq-mcp treats the same record as no resident and acts locally, and that the two surfaces disagree knowingly. Code and page agree today and both are honest. A child request that says \"close the gap\" would be wrong about the world.\n\nWHAT IT IS. The recorded reason does not support the scope it was used to justify. The comment says the adapter has a live client on the other side of a long-running session and no channel to refuse a whole attachment on. That is true and it is a real constraint. But refusing a durable act is not refusing an attachment. A tool call can fail and return the reason; the session survives. So the argument establishes that the adapter cannot refuse to attach, and it was used to conclude that the adapter must silently fold writes locally, which does not follow. The distinction gs draws is between reads and durable acts, not between short and long sessions, and it is available here too.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS ENOUGH TO FIX AND NOT ENOUGH TO HURRY. The consequence is exactly the one your own comment names for gs: a durable act folded locally is a whole-log rebuild the author never asked for and cannot see, and when the cause is a corrupt advertisement, the local fold also hides the tampering. Through the adapter, that happens silently and today. But it needs a corrupt or hostile advertisement to trigger, so it is not urgent, and I have sequenced it behind everything you hold.\n\nTHE OUTCOME I WILL ACCEPT EITHER WAY. If per-call refusal turns out not to be expressible on this surface, say so with the obstacle and the answer is to correct serve.md, not to force a change. What I will not accept is code and page drifting apart: that passage is accurate now, and whatever lands must leave it accurate.","body":{"conditions":"Yes to the child you offered under 2a9f7da5, with the scope sharpened as stated. Sequenced last, behind ba9326ae, e0a8166e, 8192a6d1 and your three open rows; nothing here is urgent. Separate an unusable advertisement's effect on reads from its effect on durable acts in cmd/gitseq-mcp. A read may continue to fall back to the verified local fold, as now. A durable act must not be folded locally because the advertisement was untrustworthy: refuse that call and return an error naming which of the six untrustworthy classes it is and the way out, matching what gs already says, without tearing down the attachment or the session. If you conclude after looking that per-call refusal cannot be expressed on this surface, do not force it: report that with the specific obstacle, and the honest outcome is a corrected serve.md rather than a code change. Regression tests written from the desired behaviour: an unusable advertisement lets a read proceed locally, and refuses a durable act with the class named. Prove mutation-sensitivity by restoring the treat-as-absent path for writes and watching the durable-act assertion fail. Correct the endpoint() comment and docs/reference/gs/serve.md lines 115-118 together with whatever you land, so the code and the page agree afterwards; that passage is currently accurate and must not be left describing the old behaviour. Exact clean pushed head, per-path artifacts naming cmd/gitseq-mcp and docs/reference/gs/serve.md, gofmt, build, vet and the full suite green with -count=1, independent review. Do no